<section class="band">
  <div class="shell">
    <h1>Supervision, consultation and training</h1>
    <p class="intro-wide">Most therapists assemble this from four providers who have never met
    and who know nothing about each other's work with you: a supervisor for the hours, someone
    else for EMDR consultation, a vendor for the ethics requirement, and a retreat if there is
    time and money left. Here it is one relationship, and that changes what each part can do.
    All four are also trauma-informed, and not in the sense that the supervisor has read about
    trauma.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band band--sand">
  <div class="shell">
    <h2>Why these belong together</h2>
    {#- The claim the cards below are evidence for. It led as a card once and
        trailed as a closing line once; as the lead-in it sets up all four. -#}
    <p class="intro-wide">NARM, Somatic Experiencing and IFS-informed EMDR frame all of it: the
    supervision hour, the consultation hour, the trail and the CEU. What you learn in one is
    usable in the next.</p>
    {#- Was four arguments for integration in the abstract, which named none of
        the four things being integrated. One card per service now, each one
        saying what the other three change about it. The claims and the rule
        citations are the same; what changed is what they are attached to. -#}
    <div class="pathways">
      <a href="/for-therapists/lpc-supervision/">
        <h3>LPC supervision, and what it pulls in</h3>
        <p>Your 3,000 hours, four a month, online anywhere in Texas. It is the only one of the
        four with an end date, and while it runs the other three change: EMDR consultation costs
        nothing, the ethics hours come from the person already supervising you, and the annual
        retreat is included.</p>
      </a>
      <a href="/for-therapists/emdr-consultation/">
        <h3>EMDR consultation, free while you are supervised</h3>
        <p>Charged by the hour on its own, and no charge for as long as you are an associate here, so
        hours toward EMDRIA certification accumulate alongside your 3,000 instead of after them.
        The consultant is the person who already knows your caseload, so no client has to be
        explained twice.</p>
      </a>
      <a href="/for-therapists/ethics-ceus/">
        <h3>Ethics CEUs, filling the half that is hard to fill</h3>
        <p>Since 2024 half of your 24 CE hours must come from a provider listed in 22 TAC
        &sect;681.140(f), and subsection (f)(6) is a licensee with supervisor status, which Reem
        holds. The subject is secondary trauma, which is the same ground supervision and the
        retreats work on, approached from the ethics side.</p>
      </a>
      <a href="/for-therapists/retreats-training/">
        <h3>Retreats and training, for what outlasts the license</h3>
        <p>Supervision ends at licensure. Certification, ethics hours and your own depletion do
        not. The retreat is where the material is you rather than a client, and associates
        supervised here are already in it.</p>
      </a>
    </div>
