<section class="band band--sand">
  <div class="shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">What the hour is actually like</p>
    <h2>What trauma-informed supervision means here</h2>
    <p class="intro-wide">Not that the supervisor knows about trauma. That the hour treats your
    own nervous system as part of the clinical picture rather than as an interruption to it.
    Three things change when it does.</p>
    <div class="cards cards--three">
      <div>
        <h3>What you bring</h3>
        <p>The cases that need the hour are the ones where you felt stupid, frozen, irritated
        with the client, or bored, and those are exactly the ones left out when bringing them
        means admitting to a reaction. They get asked for first here.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>What counts as data</h3>
        <p>Coming out of a session with your jaw tight and no memory of the last ten minutes is
        information about what happened in the room, not evidence you are bad at this. Your
        activation is often the clearest read available on a client who cannot yet say what is
        happening to them.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>How fast the hour moves</h3>
        <p>Piling on technique when you are already overwhelmed is the thing we all tell clients
        not to do. Sometimes the useful hour is one piece of understanding and nothing else, and
        the rest waits.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a class="cta cta--quiet" href="/blog/trauma-informed-supervision/">Why this matters more for associates</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

